---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { CpmCalculator } from '../../../ui/CpmCalculator';
import { getTool, relatedLive } from '../../../core/tools/registry';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd, softwareApplicationJsonLd, toolMeta } from '../../../lib/seo';
import '../../../styles/tool.css';

const tool = getTool('cpm-calculator');
if (!tool) throw new Error('tool registry entry missing');
const meta = toolMeta(tool);
const related = relatedLive(tool);
const sources = tool.sources ?? [];

const jsonLd = [
  softwareApplicationJsonLd(tool),
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Tools', path: '/tools/' },
    { name: 'CPM Calculator', path: tool.path! },
  ]),
];

const breadcrumbs = tool.breadcrumb ?? [];
---

<Base title={meta.title} description={meta.description} canonicalPath={tool.path} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((item, i) => (
        <li aria-current={i === breadcrumbs.length - 1 ? 'page' : undefined}>
          {item.path && i < breadcrumbs.length - 1 ? (
            <a href={item.path}>{item.label}</a>
          ) : (
            item.label
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h1>CPM Calculator</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Enter any two of ad spend, impressions and CPM to get the third instantly. Add clicks,
    conversions or revenue for CPC, CTR, CPA and ROAS. Free, private, no sign-up — everything runs
    in your browser.
  </p>

  <CpmCalculator client:load />

  <AdSlot slot="below-results" />

  <article class="prose">
    <section>
      <h2>What is CPM?</h2>
      <p>
        CPM — cost per mille, mille being Latin for one thousand — is the price you pay for every
        1,000 impressions your ad receives. A $10 CPM means each block of a thousand ad views costs
        ten dollars, whether or not anyone clicks. It is the standard pricing unit for display,
        video, and awareness-focused paid social campaigns; on the Google Display Network and
        YouTube you can bid directly on impressions with CPM or viewable CPM (vCPM) strategies.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same formula runs backwards: if you know your CPM and your reach target, it tells you
        the budget; if you know the budget and the rate, it tells you the reach. This calculator
        solves all three directions and also derives the metrics CPM relates to — cost per click,
        click-through rate, cost per acquisition and return on ad spend — when you supply the extra
        numbers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The formulas</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Metric</th>
              <th scope="col">Formula</th>
              <th scope="col">Solves</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>CPM</td>
              <td>(Ad spend ÷ Impressions) × 1,000</td>
              <td>What each thousand impressions cost</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Ad spend</td>
              <td>(CPM × Impressions) ÷ 1,000</td>
              <td>Total budget for a planned reach</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Impressions</td>
              <td>(Ad spend ÷ CPM) × 1,000</td>
              <td>Reach a budget buys at a known rate</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Optional inputs unlock the related metrics:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>CPC</strong> = Ad spend ÷ Clicks
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CTR</strong> = (Clicks ÷ Impressions) × 100
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CPA</strong> = Ad spend ÷ Conversions
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ROAS</strong> = Revenue ÷ Ad spend
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How to use it</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Pick what you want to calculate — CPM, ad spend, or impressions.</li>
        <li>Enter the other two values. The result appears as you type.</li>
        <li>
          Optionally add clicks, conversions or revenue to see CPC, CTR, CPA, ROAS and profit
          alongside the primary answer.
        </li>
        <li>Use "Copy results" to paste a summary into a report or media plan.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Worked example</h2>
      <p>
        A display campaign spent $2,500 and served 500,000 impressions. CPM = ($2,500 ÷ 500,000) ×
        1,000 = <strong>$5.00</strong>. If the same campaign produced 750 clicks, CPC is $3.33 and
        CTR is 0.15%. Planning backwards instead: to reach 1,000,000 impressions at a $6 CPM you
        need a $6,000 budget — (6 × 1,000,000) ÷ 1,000.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When CPM is the right lens</h2>
      <p>
        CPM measures the cost of reach, not the cost of results. It is the right primary metric for
        brand-awareness and video campaigns where being seen is the goal — Google recommends
        impression-based bidding for awareness objectives. For performance goals, CPM still matters
        but only through the chain it feeds: CPM sets the price of attention, CTR decides how much
        of that attention becomes traffic, and CPC/CPA decide whether the traffic was worth buying.
        Comparing CPM across channels without comparing the CTR and CPA behind it can mislead — a
        cheap CPM on a low-intent placement can still produce an expensive conversion.
      </p>
      <p>
        CPM rates vary widely by industry, audience, format, geography, season and placement, so
        this page deliberately does not publish "average CPM" figures — treat any single benchmark
        as context, not a target. Your own account history is the most reliable baseline.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>CPM vs CPC vs CPA vs ROAS</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Metric</th>
              <th scope="col">Answers</th>
              <th scope="col">Best for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>CPM</td>
              <td>What does a thousand impressions cost?</td>
              <td>Awareness, reach planning, display &amp; video</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>CPC</td>
              <td>What does one click cost?</td>
              <td>Traffic campaigns, search</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>CTR</td>
              <td>What share of impressions click?</td>
              <td>Creative and relevance health</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>CPA</td>
              <td>What does one conversion cost?</td>
              <td>Performance campaigns</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>ROAS</td>
              <td>How much revenue per dollar of spend?</td>
              <td>Profitability and budget decisions</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        This calculator does arithmetic, not forecasting. It cannot predict auction outcomes, and it
        treats the numbers you enter as exact. Ad platforms may report served versus viewable
        impressions differently (vCPM bills only viewable impressions), so make sure the impressions
        figure you enter matches the basis you mean. Currency selection is display-only — the math
        itself is unitless.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        Every calculation runs in your browser. Your numbers are never sent to a server, never
        logged, and never included in analytics — the tool only records that a calculation happened,
        not the values. Your draft stays in tab-scoped sessionStorage. Read the{' '}
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        {sources.map((source) => (
          <li>
            <a href={source.url} rel="noopener noreferrer">
              {source.label}
            </a>{' '}
            — verified {source.lastVerified}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>Page content last reviewed {tool.content?.lastReviewed}.</p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <AdSlot slot="end-of-content" />

  <section aria-labelledby="related-tools">
    <h2 id="related-tools">Related tools</h2>
    {related.length > 0 ? (
      <ul class="tool-cards">
        {related.map((t) => (
          <li>
            <a class="tool-card" href={t.path}>
              <h3>{t.name}</h3>
              <p>{t.description}</p>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    ) : (
      <p>More FastestAds tools are in development.</p>
    )}
  </section>
</Base>
